<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Real life: marginal revenue (INNODEMS)</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>A small business in Nakuru sells packets of yoghurt. Its total revenue, in KSh, from selling \( x \) packets in a day is</p>
\[ R(x) = {@rx@}. \]
[[jsxgraph width="460px" height="340px"]]
var P = {#pr#}, Qc = {#qc#}, Xs = {#xs#};
function R(x){ return P*x - Qc*x*x; }
var top = R(Xs);
var board = JXG.JSXGraph.initBoard(divid, {
    boundingbox: [-0.12*Xs, 1.2*top, 2.1*Xs, -0.14*top],
    axis: true, showCopyright: false, showNavigation: false,
    pan: {enabled:false}, zoom: {enabled:false}
});
board.create('functiongraph', [R, 0, 2*Xs], {strokeColor:'#1565c0', strokeWidth:2, highlight:false});
board.create('text', [1.75*Xs, -0.08*top, 'x (packets)'], {fontSize:12, fixed:true});
board.create('text', [0.02*Xs, 1.13*top, 'R (KSh)'], {fontSize:12, fixed:true});
[[/jsxgraph]]
<p>The derivative \( R'(x) \) is called the <strong>marginal revenue</strong>: the approximate extra revenue from selling one more packet when \( x \) packets have already been sold.</p>
<p>(a) Find \( R'(x) \).</p>
<div>\( R'(x) = \) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>
<p>(b) Find the marginal revenue when \( {@x1@} \) packets have been sold.</p>
<div>\( R'({@x1@}) = \) [[input:ans2]] KSh per packet [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>
<p>(c) It turns out that \( R'({@x2@}) = {@r2@} \). Which statement best describes what this means?</p>
<div>[[input:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>
<p>(d) How many packets should be sold to make the total revenue as large as possible, and what is that maximum revenue?</p>
<div>Number of packets \( = \) [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</div>
<div>Maximum revenue \( = \) KSh [[input:ans5]] [[validation:ans5]] [[feedback:prt5]]</div>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>(a)</strong> \( R'(x) = {@drx@} \) KSh per packet.</p>
<p><strong>(b)</strong> \( R'({@x1@}) = {@pr@} - {@2*qc@}({@x1@}) = {@r1@} \) KSh per packet. When \( {@x1@} \) packets have been sold, selling one more adds roughly KSh \( {@r1@} \) to the day's revenue.</p>
<p><strong>(c)</strong> \( R'({@x2@}) = {@r2@} < 0 \): when \( {@x2@} \) packets have been sold, selling one more would <em>reduce</em> total revenue by about KSh \( {@-r2@} \). The derivative is a <em>rate</em> (KSh per packet), not the revenue itself.</p>
<p><strong>(d)</strong> Revenue stops rising and starts falling where \( R'(x) = 0 \):</p>
\[ {@drx@} = 0 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad x = {@xs@}, \]
\[ R({@xs@}) = {@pr@}({@xs@}) - {@qc@}({@xs@})^2 = {@rmax@} \text{ KSh}. \]
<p>On the graph this is the top of the curve, where the tangent is horizontal. Note that \( R(x) = 0 \) at \( x = {@2*xs@} \): that is where revenue has fallen back to nothing, not where it is largest.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
    <defaultgrade>5</defaultgrade>
    <penalty>0.1</penalty>
